    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe syntax error at line 209 (unexpected token 'p' while parsing 'pred precedes') is triggered because the function firstPendingFIFO[s: State] is incorrectly defined as a function, but its logic requires quantification over Requests to select a unique \"first pending\" request. In Alloy 6, functions must return a deterministic value and cannot use 'lone' quantification in their bodies to select something that may not be unique; such logic should be modeled as a predicate that relates s and r (i.e., \"r is the first pending request in s\"). The use of 'lone r: Request | ...' as a function body is syntactically problematic in Alloy 6, as function bodies must be single expressions, not quantified formulas or set comprehensions. Previous attempts tried to reformat the function body and add semicolons but did not address this fundamental mismatch in Alloy's abstraction: this is a predicate, not a function.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nRedefine firstPendingFIFO as a predicate rather than a function, so that the logic about the first pending request is expressed relationally and not as an illegal function body.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Delete the existing function definition of firstPendingFIFO[s: State].\n- Step 2: Replace it with a predicate definition that relates a State s and a Request r, expressing \"r is the first pending request in s\":\n      pred firstPendingFIFO[s: State, r: Request] {\n          s.reqStatus[r] = Pending and\n          no r2: Request | s.reqStatus[r2] = Pending and precedes[r2, r]\n      }\n- Step 3: Update all usages of firstPendingFIFO[s] elsewhere in the model (such as in fact NormalModeFIFO and assertion assertNormalModeFIFO) to use the new predicate form. For example, replace:\n      let first = firstPendingFIFO[s] |\n      all r: Request | ... r = first ...\n  with:\n      some rFirst: Request | firstPendingFIFO[s, rFirst] and ... r = rFirst ...\n- Step 4: Ensure every use now calls firstPendi